---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import CodeTabs from '../../components/CodeTabs.astro';
import Note from '../../components/Note.astro';
import { groups } from '../../data/ruleGroups';
import { rules } from '../../data/rules';

const phpBad = String.raw`final class Account
{
    public int $balance = 0;
}

$account->balance = $account->balance - 500;`;

const phpGood = String.raw`final class Account
{
    private function __construct(private Money $balance) {}

    public function withdraw(Money $amount): self
    {
        if ($this->balance->isLessThan($amount)) {
            throw new InsufficientFunds($amount);
        }

        return new self($this->balance->subtract($amount));
    }
}

$account = $account->withdraw(Money::fromCents(500));`;

const csBad = String.raw`public sealed class Account
{
    public decimal Balance { get; set; }
}

account.Balance -= 500m;`;

const csGood = String.raw`public sealed class Account
{
    private readonly Money balance;

    private Account(Money balance) => this.balance = balance;

    public Account Withdraw(Money amount) =>
        balance.IsLessThan(amount)
            ? throw new InsufficientFunds(amount)
            : new Account(balance.Subtract(amount));
}`;

const javaBad = String.raw`public final class Account {
    private long balance;

    public long getBalance() { return balance; }
    public void setBalance(long balance) { this.balance = balance; }
}

account.setBalance(account.getBalance() - 500);`;

const javaGood = String.raw`public final class Account {
    private final Money balance;

    private Account(Money balance) { this.balance = balance; }

    public Account withdraw(Money amount) {
        if (balance.isLessThan(amount))
            throw new InsufficientFunds(amount);

        return new Account(balance.subtract(amount));
    }
}`;

const tsBad = String.raw`export class Account {
  public balance = 0;
}

account.balance -= 500;`;

const tsGood = String.raw`export class Account {
  private constructor(private readonly balance: Money) {}

  withdraw(amount: Money): Account {
    if (this.balance.isLessThan(amount)) {
      throw new InsufficientFunds(amount);
    }

    return new Account(this.balance.subtract(amount));
  }
}`;

const phpTwoVars = String.raw`// Three instance variables: the concept has no name yet.
final class Service
{
    public function __construct(
        private ServiceName $name,
        private ServiceHost $host,
        private ServicePort $port,
    ) {}
}

// The concept is an endpoint. Name it, and the class drops to two fields.
final class Service
{
    private function __construct(
        private ServiceName $name,
        private Endpoint $endpoint,
    ) {}
}`;

const phpResult = String.raw`// Failures are values, not nulls.
final class WithdrawMoney
{
    public function __invoke(AccountId $id, Money $amount): Result
    {
        $account = $this->accounts->find($id);

        return $account->canWithdraw($amount)
            ? Result::success($account->withdraw($amount))
            : Result::failure(new InsufficientFunds($amount));
    }
}`;
---

<Doc
  title="The forty rules"
  kicker="Track O — Own your Discipline"
  description="The 40 measurable OTAKU rules, grouped by pillar, with the thresholds, the primary metric and the examples that are most often misread."
  path="/guide/rules"
>
  <p class="lede">
    Forty rules, each with a threshold. They are mechanical for a reason: a rule that
    depends on taste cannot be reviewed, automated or argued with.
  </p>

  <p>
    Read the rule, then check whether your code passes. When in doubt, choose the more
    restrictive option — that is the tie-breaker the philosophy prescribes.
  </p>

  <h2>The primary metric</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Cyclomatic complexity ≤ 5 per method.</strong> This is the main instrument,
    not a decoration. It counts the independent paths a reader must hold in their head
    at once: every <code>if</code>, every loop, every boolean operator adds one.
  </p>

  <p>
    A method at 6 is rejected, even if it is short and beautifully indented. The other
    rules exist to keep this number low — guard clauses remove branches, extraction
    moves them into names, polymorphism replaces <code>switch</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>All forty</h2>

  {groups.map((group) => (
    <section class="rule-group">
      <h3 class="rule-group__title">
        <span class="rule-group__pillar">{group.pillar}</span>
        {group.title.en}
      </h3>
      <ol class="rules">
        {group.ids.map((id) => (
          <li value={id}>{rules.find((rule) => rule.id === id)?.text.en}</li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>The rule that is most often misread</h2>

  <p>
    Rule 25 forbids getters, setters and public properties. The usual mistake is to
    delete the getter and make the field public — which is worse, because now the
    invariant has no guardian at all.
  </p>

  <p>
    The point is not visibility. It is <strong>ownership</strong>: the object must be
    the one that operates on its own attributes. Asking for state and changing it
    outside is procedural programming with classes.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="Rule 25 — ownership, not visibility"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'php', label: 'PHP — wrong', code: phpBad },
      { id: 'cs', label: 'PHP — right', code: phpGood },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Both wrong examples below are equivalent: one exposes the field, the other exposes
    the field through methods. Neither protects the balance.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="The same mistake in four languages"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'cs', label: 'C# — wrong', code: csBad },
      { id: 'java', label: 'Java — wrong', code: javaBad },
      { id: 'ts', label: 'TypeScript — wrong', code: tsBad },
      { id: 'ts', label: 'TypeScript — right', code: tsGood },
    ]}
  />

  <Note label="Read it as" variant="warn">
    <p>
      “No getters and setters” means “the class asks itself the questions it was
      created to answer”. A <code>withdraw()</code> is a question. A
      <code>setBalance()</code> is a confession that the class has no idea what it is.
    </p>
  </Note>

  <h2>The rule that is most often ignored</h2>

  <p>
    Rule 9 — at most two instance variables. Three fields usually mean three
    responsibilities sharing a constructor, or one unnamed concept.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="Rule 9 — name the concept"
    tabs={[{ id: 'php', label: 'PHP', code: phpTwoVars }]}
  />

  <p>
    The second version is not shorter by accident. <code>Endpoint</code> is the name
    the codebase was missing, and it is now the place where host and port validate each
    other.
  </p>

  <h2>Rules that change a signature</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>What it removes</th><th>What it adds</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>10, 11 — two parameters</td><td>long constructors and methods</td><td>parameter objects with names</td></tr>
        <tr><td>12 — two methods per interface</td><td>broad “service” interfaces</td><td>role interfaces, one per client</td></tr>
        <tr><td>34 — no null</td><td><code>null</code> as an undocumented third state</td><td>Result/Either or a domain exception</td></tr>
        <tr><td>39 — every public method in an interface</td><td>concrete coupling</td><td>a contract you can fake in tests</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <CodeTabs
    title="Rule 34 — failure as a value"
    tabs={[{ id: 'php', label: 'PHP', code: phpResult }]}
  />

  <h2>Measuring the primary metric</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Language</th><th>Tool</th><th>Signal</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>PHP</td><td>PHP_CodeSniffer (CyclomaticComplexity), PHPStan, phpMetrics</td><td>fail above 5</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C#</td><td>Roslyn analyzers (CA1502), NDepend</td><td>warn/fail above 5</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Java</td><td>PMD (CyclomaticComplexity), Checkstyle, SonarQube</td><td>fail above 5</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C++</td><td>clang-tidy <code>readability-function-cognitive-complexity</code>, lizard</td><td>fail above 5</td></tr>
        <tr><td>TypeScript</td><td>ESLint <code>complexity</code>, ts-arch</td><td>fail above 5</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Set the threshold in the pipeline, not in a wiki. A metric that is only visible in
    review is already dead.
  </p>

  <h2>Applying this to an existing codebase</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>Turn on the complexity check in report mode and count violations.</li>
    <li>Pick the worst file, extract until the top method is below 5.</li>
    <li>Lock that file with a baseline: it may not get worse.</li>
    <li>Repeat weekly. The baseline shrinks or the number of violations freezes — both are progress.</li>
  </ol>

  <Note label="Why this order">
    <p>
      Complexity first, because it is the cheapest to measure and the most correlated
      with defects. Size and naming come next; they are consequences of the same
      decision, not independent virtues.
    </p>
  </Note>
</Doc>
